<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cotyora</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kotyora</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cotyora (Kotyora) was an ancient Greek colony of Sinope on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near modern Ordu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-thousand">Xenophon's Ten Thousand</a> rested there in 400 BCE on their march home. It was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-i-of-pontus">Pharnaces I of Pontus</a>, who moved part of its population to his new city of Pharnacia, and it declined thereafter.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotyora.jpg" alt="Cotyora" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Gürsakal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ordu,_Turkey.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
